diagnosis_text:
A thorough inspection of every layer in the simple_bgp topology finds no fault.
The reported symptom — lost connectivity between pc1 (195.11.14.2) and pc2
(200.1.1.2) — could not be reproduced: a live ping traversed the full path with
0% packet loss and sub-2 ms RTT.

Physical / L2: both WAN-side interfaces are fully operational.
router1 eth0 (193.10.11.1/24) and router2 eth0 (193.10.11.2/24) both show
<BROADCAST,MULTICAST,UP,LOWER_UP> with no errors or drops. No cabling issue
exists; bouncing either interface would be unnecessary and disruptive.

BGP control plane: the eBGP session between AS 1 (router1) and AS 2 (router2)
is in Established state, up for over two minutes, with a hold-time of 180 s and
regular keepalives exchanged every 60 s. "Connections established: 1, dropped:
0" on both peers. Each router correctly carries the remote prefix: router1 holds
200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2 and router2 holds 195.11.14.0/24 via 193.10.11.1.
No NOTIFICATION messages appear in either log.

Host routing: pc1 routes via gateway 195.11.14.1 (router1 eth1) and pc2 via
200.1.1.1 (router2 eth1) — both correct.

The FRR logs show only a transient "nexthop_set failed / resetting connection"
event at the very first second of startup (before the TCP session was fully
established), which resolved on its own. The network has been healthy since
convergence.

Root cause: there is no fault — the NOC's premise that the WAN link is down is
incorrect; the link is up and fully operational.

Corrective action: none required. Advise the NOC that the reported outage was
either a momentary transient during lab startup or a false alarm, and that no
device restart or cable check is warranted.
